CODE STYLE GUIDELINES:

General:
The code is written in Python.
Code must be easy to understand, maintain, test, deploy, and scale.
Code must be written as simply as possible, but no simpler.
Code must be easy for humans to read.
Favor explicitness over magic.

Design
Prefer OOP, but use simple functions when OOP adds no clarity.
Prefer composition over inheritance.
Use dependency injection to implement Dependency Inversion.
Follow DRY principles (don't repeat yourself).
Follow SOLID principles:
* Single Responsibility
* Open/Closed
* Liskov Substitution
* Interface Segregation
* Dependency Inversion
Do not introduce abstractions without at least two concrete use cases.

Scalability:
Avoid global state.
Avoid tight coupling.
External dependencies must be abstracted behind interfaces.
Domain logic must not depend on frameworks.

Standards:
Use PEP 8 for Python code
Format with black.
Lint with ruff (or flake8).
Sort imports with isort.
Use type hints for all function signatures.
Use mypy (or pyright) for static type checking.
Avoid Any unless absolutely necessary.
Prefer explicit return types.
Public classes and functions must have docstrings, use Google-style.

Testing
Use pytest for testing.
No business logic inside controllers or frameworks.
Use dependency injection to mock external systems.
Tests must be deterministic (no random sleeps, no real network calls).

Logging:
Use structured logging (never print in production code).
Never swallow exceptions silently.
Raise domain-specific exceptions.
Fail fast on invalid state.

Naming
Classes → PascalCase
Functions → snake_case
Constants → UPPER_CASE
Private members → _leading_underscore

Configuration
Secrets must only be provided via environment variables.
Secrets must never be hardcoded or committed.
Non-secret configuration must use structured settings management.
Use pydantic settings models for application configuration.